//BUG="a new variable is in scope inside the expression that first assigns it"
//ERR any
//MSG="Variable with name x not found"

// `normalize_block` (src/compiler/normalizer/mod.rs) handles an assignment to
// an unknown name by creating the variable first and normalizing the value
// afterwards. The value can therefore mention the variable that is being
// declared. With the type pinned by the hint, everything type-checks, and the
// statement compiles to
//
//     var0 = func1(var0, 1);
//
// in a function whose locals are declared without an initial value. The
// program reads an uninitialised `int`, adds one, and prints whatever was on
// the stack — a different number from run to run and from -O0 to -O2.
//
// Without the hint the same line is rejected, though for an unrelated
// reason: the type of `x` is unknown, so `x + 1` reports "Multiple candidates
// found for this function call".
//
// A name used before anything was assigned to it is the mistake
// 02_variables/50e_unknown_variable.cpl exists for, and this case expects
// the same message. The fix is to normalize the right-hand side before the
// new variable is added to the symbol table.

fn main
    x: i32 = x + 1
    out "{x}"
